==Old Norse==
{{was fwotd|2013|February|5|lang=non}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|non|gem-pro|*hundaradą}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*hundą}} (< {{etyl|ine-pro|non}} {{m|ine-pro|*ḱm̥tóm}}) + {{m|gem-pro|*radą||count}}. Cognate to {{etyl|en|-}} {{term|hundred||[[short hundred]], 100|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/hundrað/|lang=non}}

===Number===
{{non-noun|n}}

# a [[long hundred]] (120)

====Usage notes====
Christianity introduced the short hundred (100), but the long hundred remained in use for a long time even after that, during which time hundreds were sometimes distinguished as {{term|heil||whole|lang=non}} or {{term|tólfræð||twelve-tenned, duodecimal|lang=non}} (for 120) or {{term|tíræð||ten-tenned, decimal|lang=non}} (for 100).

===See also===
* [[tíu-tíu]] {{qualifier|[[teenty]], one [[short hundred]], 100}}

===References===
* Richard Cleasby, Gudbrand Vigfusson, ''An Icelandic-English Dictionary'' (1874)

